$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The seizure</h2>
<p>Officers of the Directorate of Revenue Intelligence stopped a pick-up van near Rajendra Nagar Terminal, Patna. Inside were 7,500 bottles of Eskuf cough syrup, a codeine phosphate preparation, travelling under transport documents that named a Kolkata consignor and a Patna consignee. The consignee&rsquo;s drug licence, it turned out, had been cancelled. The driver &mdash; the petitioner &mdash; was arrested and had been in judicial custody since 27 May 2026 in DRI Case No. 332 of 2026, charged under Sections 8, 21(c) and 29 of the NDPS Act.</p>

<h2>The statutory route to bail</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Start with the notification.</strong> Notification S.O. 826(E) of 14 November 1985, issued under the NDPS Act, read with entry 2 of Rule 52A of the NDPS Rules, exempts codeine preparations established in therapeutic practice if they contain no more than 100 mg per dosage unit or no more than 2.5% concentration in undivided preparations.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Test the seizure against it.</strong> The Eskuf syrup was below the 2.5% concentration ceiling and met the therapeutic-practice condition. At the threshold, it was not a &ldquo;narcotic drug&rdquo; for prosecution purposes.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Regulatory confirmation.</strong> A 2005 letter of the Drug Controller General of India places such syrups under Schedule H of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules &mdash; a prescription-drug regime, policed by the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, not the NDPS Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Only then ask about quantity.</strong> Because the Act did not bite, the small-versus-commercial quantity inquiry under <em>Hira Singh</em> was unnecessary. Quantity grading is a sentencing architecture inside the Act, not a gateway into it.</div>
</div>

<h2>Possession and the hired driver</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s second strand was factual. The petitioner drove a consigned load under documents prepared by others. Conscious possession &mdash; the mental element the NDPS Act requires &mdash; was not established against a hired driver on this record. The petitioner had also argued non-compliance with Sections 103 and 105 of the BNSS during search and seizure. With the threshold point and the possession point both in his favour, continued custody could not be justified.</p>

<h2>What the decision signals</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Position after this ruling</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Codeine syrup within limits</td><td>Outside the NDPS Act; regulated as a Schedule H drug under the Drugs and Cosmetics regime</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Licence irregularities in the chain</td><td>A cancelled consignee licence raises Drugs and Cosmetics Act issues; it does not convert the cargo into contraband</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Transport workers</td><td>Conscious possession must be shown against drivers and loaders individually, not inferred from presence at the wheel</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Institutional direction</td><td>The DGP, Bihar was directed to sensitise police personnel, signalling the court&rsquo;s concern at repeated misapplication of the Act to exempt preparations</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The court distinguished contrary High Court decisions relied on by the DRI, anchoring its analysis in the notification text. For practitioners, the sequencing is the takeaway: in any codeine-preparation prosecution, the exemption analysis comes first, and everything else &mdash; quantity, Section 37 rigours, even bail conditions &mdash; follows from it.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Patna High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice; whether a given preparation falls within the exemption depends on its composition and the evidence in each case.</div>
